      `children:[`

        `// ABSTRACT`
        `h1("Abstract"),`
        `b("This paper presents a doctoral-level investigation into the life, works, and enduring legacy of Johannes Trithemius (1462-1516) — Benedictine Abbot of Sponheim and Wurzburg, polymath, bibliographer, demonologist, cryptographer, and the figure posterity would name the Black Abbot. We examine Trithemius as the intellectual progenitor of systematic cryptography whose methods anticipated steganographic encoding, polyalphabetic cipher design, information layering, and networked covert communication by centuries. The paper conducts close analysis of the Steganographia's three books and the cryptographic machinery concealed beneath their angelic invocation architecture; his demonological treatises — above all the Liber octo quaestionum, with its doctrine of witches who shapeshift, conjure weather, and swear lasting oaths of obedience to demonic masters. We situate these writings within the full manuscript ecosystem of the period: Ulrich Molitor's De lamiis et pythonicis mulieribus (1489), the first illustrated witchcraft treatise, with its pioneering woodcuts of weather witches, beast-headed sabbat riders, and storm-summoning; the Munich Necromancer's Manual (CLM 849, c.1440-1470), containing the Mirror of Floron, the Bond of Solomon, and demonic conjurations for locating buried treasure; the Florentine necromantic manual (Biblioteca Medicea Laurenziana, MS Plut. 89 sup. 38), whose self-description as a libro de officiis antedates Trithemius's own naming of a Liber officiorum by less than two decades and shares experimental material with the Munich codex; the Hermetic lineage of Ludovico Lazzarelli (1447-1500) and his master Giovanni Mercurio da Correggio, whose Palm Sunday 1484 messianic entry into Rome Trithemius himself recorded and whose Christian-Hermetic synthesis via the Crater Hermetis was the theological current flowing directly into Cornelius Agrippa's De Occulta Philosophia — the work Trithemius authorized; and the literary crystallization of the Faustian archetype, from Trithemius's own 1507 letter denouncing the historical Georgius Sabellicus Faustus junior as a fraudulent necromancer, through the 1587 Historia von D. Johann Fausten, to Christopher Marlowe's The Tragicall History of the Life and Death of Doctor Faustus (c.1592), in which the diabolical compact Trithemius simultaneously practiced, theorized, and condemned becomes the defining myth of Western modernity. Finally, we trace the transmission lineage from Trithemius through Vigenere, Cardano, Porta, Alberti, and into modern computing and internet security, arguing that the Black Abbot's architecture of layered hidden communication is the founding protocol of Western cryptographic science.",{noIndent:true}),`
        `div(),`

        `// TOC`
        `h1("Table of Contents"),`
        `b("I.      Introduction: The Black Abbot and His Paradox",{noIndent:true}),`
        `b("II.     Historical and Biographical Context",{noIndent:true}),`
        `b("III.    The Corpus: Works, Later Editions, and the Liber octo quaestionum",{noIndent:true}),`
        `b("IV.     Witches, Shapeshifting, and Weather Magic in Trithemian Demonology",{noIndent:true}),`
        `b("V.      Molitor's De Lamiis (1489): The Witches Sabbat and its Visual Culture",{noIndent:true}),`
        `b("VI.     The Munich Necromancer's Manual (CLM 849): Floron, Treasure, Demonic Conjuration",{noIndent:true}),`
        `b("VII.    The Florentine Necromantic Manual (MS Plut. 89 sup. 38): The Medici Network",{noIndent:true}),`
        `b("VIII.   Lazzarelli, Correggio, and the Hermetic Current",{noIndent:true}),`
